ARP capture analysis

1. First ARP destination MAC address

The first ARP Request was sent to ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff.
This is the Ethernet broadcast address. R1 used broadcast because it knew
R2's IPv4 address, 10.10.10.2, but did not yet know R2's MAC address.

2. Request and response MAC observations

In the ARP Request:
- Sender: 10.10.10.1 / c4:01:53:7c:00:00
- Target IP: 10.10.10.2
- Target hardware address: 00:00:00:00:00:00

The all-zero target hardware address means that the requested MAC address
was still unknown.

In the ARP Reply:
- Sender: 10.10.10.2 / c4:02:36:58:00:00
- Target: 10.10.10.1 / c4:01:53:7c:00:00

The reply was unicast directly to R1. It informed R1 that 10.10.10.2 is
reachable through MAC address c4:02:36:58:00:00. The ICMP Echo Requests and
Replies that followed used these learned unicast MAC addresses.

3. ARP spoofing logic

ARP does not authenticate the IP-to-MAC information contained in replies.
An attacker on the same local network may send forged ARP information that
associates a trusted IPv4 address with an incorrect MAC address. If a host
accepts and caches that false mapping, traffic may be redirected or disrupted.

Defensive controls include Dynamic ARP Inspection, DHCP Snooping, monitoring
unexpected IP-to-MAC changes, network segmentation, and end-to-end encryption.

